**Abstract Class vs Interface:**

| Feature | Abstract Class | Interface |
|---------|----------------|-----------|
| Instantiation | Cannot be instantiated | Cannot be instantiated |
| Method implementation | Can have both abstract and concrete | All methods abstract (except default in Java 8+) |
| Fields | Can have instance variables | Only static final constants (Java) |
| Multiple inheritance | Single inheritance only | Multiple interfaces allowed |
| Access modifiers | All types allowed | Public by default |
| When to use | Common base with shared code | Contract/behavior definition |

**Benefits of Abstraction:**
- Hides implementation complexity
- Reduces code duplication
- Improves maintainability
- Enforces design contracts
- Supports polymorphism

**Constructors** are special methods called when an object is created. **Destructors** (finalizers) clean up resources when an object is destroyed.

```python
# Python
class Student:
    # Class variable (shared across all instances)
    school = "XYZ High School"

    # Constructor
    def __init__(self, name, age, grade=None):
        self.name = name          # Instance variable
        self.age = age
        self.grade = grade if grade else "Not assigned"
        print(f"Student {self.name} created")

    # Destructor (called when object is garbage collected)
    def __del__(self):
        print(f"Student {self.name} destroyed")

    # Factory method (alternative constructor)
    @classmethod
    def from_dict(cls, data):
        return cls(data['name'], data['age'], data.get('grade'))

# Creating objects
student1 = Student("Alice", 15)           # Default/parameterized
student2 = Student("Bob", 16, "10th")     # Parameterized
student3 = Student.from_dict({"name": "Charlie", "age": 14, "grade": "9th"})

# Copy not automatic in Python - use copy module
import copy
student_copy = copy.copy(student1)  # Shallow copy
```

